Research decision memo — Stage 7
Title: Random Matrix Diagnostics of Endogenous Correlations
Status: NOT STARTED

Central hypothesis: Do spectral patterns of model-generated financial returns identify economically meaningful common risk beyond sampling noise?

Feasibility prior:
Direct as a statistical diagnostic of equilibrium outcomes, but random matrix theory by itself is not an optimal-choice or market-clearing mechanism.

Core economic distinction:
Independent asset returns and conventional factor models at the same sample size

Conditions to check:
1. Finite-sample spectral measures
2. Null distribution under documented independence assumptions
3. Model-generated covariance and risk network functions

Pass / fail criterion:
Retain only diagnostically or predictively informative spectral statistics; no artificial structural claim.

The project reports negative feasibility results when a kinetic, entropy, adaptive-trading, or heuristic-firm mechanism cannot be reconciled with optimization and equilibrium. Do not silently weaken rational expectations or drop clearing equations.

Evidence status: no derived theorems, numerical solutions or validated simulations committed yet.
